{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Ideal Quotient.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Ideal Quotient.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"ideal quotient ideal quotient","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Ideal Quotient.md","fileName":"Ideal Quotient.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Ideal Quotient","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-13 19:40\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[Prime Ideals and Noetherian Algebra]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Prime Ideals and Noetherian Algebra","Saturation of an Ideal","commutativealgebra.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Ideal Quotient.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Ideal Quotient.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"ideal quotient ideal quotient","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Ideal Quotient.md","fileName":"Ideal Quotient.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Ideal Quotient","heading":"Ideal Quotient","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"For ideals I and J in a ring R, the ideal quotient (I:J) consists of elements r such that rJ is contained in I. It measures which multipliers force all of J into I.\n\nThe special case (0:J) is the annihilator of J. Replacing J by successive powers produces the [[Saturation of an Ideal]], which collects elements that enter I after multiplication by a sufficiently high power.","tags":["baby"],"links":["Prime Ideals and Noetherian Algebra","Saturation of an Ideal","commutativealgebra.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Ideal Quotient.md::2","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Ideal Quotient.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"ideal quotient ideal quotient","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Ideal Quotient.md","fileName":"Ideal Quotient.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Ideal Quotient","heading":"References